## 🔹 Naive Approach in C++
Same idea:
1. Copy `nums2` into `nums1` (after the first `m` elements).  
2. Sort the combined array using `std::sort` (way easier than C’s `qsort`).  

---

### ✅ C++ Code

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm> // for sort

using namespace std;

void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int n) {
    // Step 1: Copy nums2 elements into nums1 after the first m elements
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        nums1[m + i] = nums2[i];
    }

    // Step 2: Sort the entire nums1
    sort(nums1.begin(), nums1.end());
}

int main() {
    vector<int> nums1 = {1,2,3,0,0,0};
    vector<int> nums2 = {2,5,6};
    int m = 3, n = 3;

    merge(nums1, m, nums2, n);

    for (int x : nums1) {
        cout << x << " ";
    }
    cout << endl; // Output: 1 2 2 3 5 6

    return 0;
}
```

